cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software, please cite it as below."
type: software
title: "RF Drone Detector: passive RF drone detection with synthetic drone signals on real background IQ data"
authors:
  - family-names: Kim
    given-names: Jeonghwan
    affiliation: Independent researcher
repository-code: "https://github.com/sinkei9456/rf-drone-detector"
license: AGPL-3.0
version: 2.1.0
date-released: 2026-11-24
keywords:
  - drone detection
  - counter-UAS
  - RF signal detection
  - software-defined radio
  - HackRF
  - spectrogram
  - synthetic training data
  - real background
  - YOLO
abstract: >-
  A HackRF One listens to 20 MHz of the 2.4 GHz band, every 20 ms of IQ samples
  becomes a 640 x 640 spectrogram, and a YOLO26s detector draws a box around the
  drone video link. Training images are synthesized at the IQ level from 303
  drone signal segments of 20 drone models in four public datasets, so no image
  is labeled by hand. Version 2.1 holds the experiments of the paper "Synthetic
  Drone Signals on Real Background IQ Data for Low-Cost SDR Drone Detection in
  Crowded Environments". Forty models trained on park noise with generated Wi-Fi
  and Bluetooth bursts (four interference densities, five folds by product
  family, two seeds) flagged 98-100 % of the drone-off images at a
  station concourse and in a subway car as drones. Models trained on IQ data
  recorded with the drone off at one of these sites, with the same drone
  signals, reached 86.8-93.7 % recall and 2.5 % and 22.5 % false positive rate
  at the other site, for a DJI Neo 2 that was never used for training. All HackRF
  data were recorded with the 20 MHz receive filter. The hackathon version
  (v1.1) is kept in the folder v1/.
